                    "patching_rect": [ 286.0, 7822.357016393449, 280.0, 36.0 ],
                    "text": ";\r\nmax launch_browser https://youtu.be/jPBzkMkQlq4"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "hidden": 1,
                    "id": "obj-95",
                    "linecount": 2,
                    "maxclass": "message",
                    "numinlets": 2,
                    "numoutlets": 1,
                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                    "patching_rect": [ 651.0, 7554.448813268449, 465.0, 36.0 ],
                    "text": ";\r\nmax launch_browser https://www.nime.org/proceedings/2020/nime2020_paper47.pdf"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-92",
                    "linecount": 8,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6614.448813268449, 628.0, 117.0 ],
                    "text": "When building more complex patches, pay close attention to namespaces utilized by the D4's sends and receives. The core D4-related sends and receives start with d4. Others utilize the bus name as the starting point. All further words are connected with hyphens (-) to create a single word. Subpatches having multiple words in their title use underscore (_) instead of spaces. Lastly, all the abstractions in the D4 library are hierarchical. They all start with D4 (a notable exceptions are jit.* abstractions that do not need to apply to just D4), and are followed by a series of words that increasingly narrow the abstraction's focus. For instance, D4.mask.editor tells us that this abstraction is dealing with the mask, and that it is an editor widget. D4.demo.sonification.data.solar.binaural shows it is a demo of a sonification of solar data using D4's binaural output, etc."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-93",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6593.448813268449, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Naming Conventions"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-90",
                    "linecount": 6,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6502.448813268449, 632.0, 89.0 ],
                    "text": "Note that most abstractions can be embedded GUI-less. Some, however, will still have a large and unnecessary computational overhead. As a result, most abstractions are further segmented into sub-abstractions that can be used independently. This is the case with a number of demos, including the aforesaid D4.demo.mask.sensel&webcam and D4.demo.sonification.data.solar.binaural that utilize D4.mask.calculator without the GUI elements of the D4.mask.editor. Similarly, the D4.player, D4.mask.renderer and others allow GUI-less embedding while providing necessary inlets and outlets."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-91",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6481.448813268449, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Embedding Spatial Mask and Other Features without the GUI"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-88",
                    "linecount": 10,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6321.448813268449, 635.0, 144.0 ],
                    "text": "By default, the GUI-based D4.mask.editor and the D4.mask.calculator abstraction responsible for the actual calculation of the mask inside it utilize the jit.speaker.cell abstraction to calculate the mask area for each loudspeaker (note that there is also a single-threaded variant called D4.mask.calculator.single that can be substituted for a deterministic manipulation of the mask). The jit.speaker.cell can be replaced by other approaches to calculating the same. D4 comes with also jit.speaker.cell.norm which normalizes the area around the loudspeakers to ensure that each loudspeaker covers exactly the same amount of space, regardless its position. This may result in situations where such areas among adjacent loudspeakers may overlap, or there may be a \"blind spot\", or an area where there can be no sound generated using the mask. This in some ways mimics the Huygens principle that explores windowed audio propagation and with each loudspeaker serving as a window. As is the case with the rest of the D4 library, jit.speaker.cell can be further edited and customized to provide additional approaches to computing the spatial mask."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-89",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6300.448813268449, 630.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Customizing Per-Loudspeaker Spatial Mask Area"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-70",
                    "linecount": 24,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5960.448813268449, 635.0, 338.0 ],
                    "text": "As evidenced in the D4.demo.sonification.data.solar.binaural (see the D4 Demos section below for additional information on this and other D4 projects), custom loudspeaker calibrations allow for dummy layers and loudspeakers that can simulate dead spots, quiet certain parts of the space (e.g. in a hemispherical dome sounds below the equatorial plane may be gradually faded out to simulate sound going below the physical plane towards the inaudible hemisphere), or enhance the spatial mask of layers that may otherwise take on too much of the elevation and thus result in weak spot-based mask.\n\nFor instance, imagine a calibration that has 3 layers, one at 0°, one at 40°, and one at 80°. In this setup, the first layer will be also responsible for all sounds below the azimuth of 0°, effectively resulting in a sound being virtually projected across the floor (e.g. a sound transitioning from 0° azimuth and 0° elevation, towards 0° azimuth -90° elevation, and eventually 0° azimuth -180° elevation will, due to lack of layers below linearly pan across the location of 0° azimuth and 0° elevation to 180° azimuth 0° elevation). This also means that for the loudspeakers on the first 0° elevation layer to have their mask at maximum possible amplitude, they will need to have the quadrant around them all the way down to -90° elevation fully filled. This will require a much greater radius and mask area as opposed to loudspeakers in between the first 0° elevation and third 80° elevation layer. While similar issue is also true for the 80° third layer, there is barely 10° elevation above them and as such loudspeakers in the third layer are not affected by the same problem. One way to address this is to create a dummy layer at -40° elevation which will thus make the 0° layer mask area consistent to other layers, while also allowing the sound to fade out, rather than virtually pan across the speace.\n\nLastly, note that while general calibrations sent via the d4-setup send will affect all instances of the D4.calc~, it is also possible to set up localized calibrations that affect only specific D4.calc~ instances. Such sends can be sent to <bus-name>-setup. This can be particularly useful in optimizing the number of outputs or utilizing only a specific subset of loudspeakers, e.g. to render an unusual trajectory, or render reflections. Multiple instances of the D4.calc~ can be then coordinated by passing them the same azimuth and elevation values."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-87",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5939.448813268449, 630.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Custom Calibrations"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-59",
                    "linecount": 4,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5875.448813268449, 630.0, 62.0 ],
                    "text": "Make sure to explore the ramp up and ramp down values of the D4.calc~ abstraction. These enable unusual effects associated with source motion. Of particular interest may be a long ramp down value (e.g. 2,000ms) which results in a comet-like trail behind the sound source. When properly calibrated, the said tail may help listeners more easily pinpoint and continually track a moving source."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-9",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5854.448813268449, 630.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Motion Blur"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-82",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 273.5, 5693.588989868891, 46.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Traces",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "handoff": "",
                    "id": "obj-86",
                    "maxclass": "ubutton",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 4,
                    "outlettype": [ "bang", "bang", "", "int" ],
                    "parameter_enable": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 275.5, 5697.088989868891, 40.0, 13.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "hidden": 1,
                    "id": "obj-75",
                    "linecount": 2,
                    "maxclass": "message",
                    "numinlets": 2,
                    "numoutlets": 1,
                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                    "patching_rect": [ 364.0, 5658.448813268449, 280.0, 36.0 ],
                    "text": ";\r\nmax launch_browser https://youtu.be/jPBzkMkQlq4"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-72",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5658.448813268449, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Threading and Optimization"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-73",
                    "linecount": 12,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5680.448813268449, 637.0, 172.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 library, when coupled with a high density loudspeaker array can quickly become computationally intensive. This was particularly evident in the 2019 artistic work Traces which pushed Max and the Unity-based Locus motion capture library to their limits with over 25K DSP calls and 8K audio streams being mixed down to 130 physical outputs live using 21.3ms latency (1024 sample buffer at 48KHz). This project was made possible through a careful use of Max's poly~ object that allows for toggling of DSP processing in individual loudspeakers based on whether that particular loudspeaker in a given bus has any meaningful output. D4 library provides an example of such an implementation in the D4.calc.poly~-example patch. It is coupled by the D4.calc.poly~ abstraction that uses D4.cell.poly~ for each of its outputs. The key to this implementation is the D4.cell.poly~ that leverages poly~'s ability to suspend its DSP computation.\n\nSimialarly, note that the D4.mask.calculator abstraction responsible for calculating per loudspeaker mask offers two versions: a multithreaded one (default), as well as the single-threaded D4.mask.calculator.single."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-71",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 4270.088989868891, 629.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Automating Other Parameters"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-69",
                    "linecount": 9,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 4292.088989868891, 629.0, 131.0 ],
                    "text": "Other automation parameters include azimuth, elevation, radius (a.k.a. spread), gain, ramp up, and ramp down. This data can be rendered using the D4.timeline.editor widget that resembles a DAW track with a 3D visualization of the ensuing source. Similar to the D4.mask.renderer, it allows for providing scaling of the timeline and shows the audio waveform (the waveform is visible only when the source file is not being looped). Once again, the D4.timeline.editor can be used as an embedded abstraction or a self-standing widget. Either way, in order for it to work, its bus needs to be specified. Once the automation is created using DAW-like controls (click on the \"help\" button for additional information and instructions), it can be also rendered into a supporting coll file that is named <bus-name>.timeline.render.coll. One such example included with the D4 library is the test.timeline.render.coll file that is designed to work in conjunction with the aforesaid spatial mask coll file and the D4.calc~-example."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-68",
                    "linecount": 26,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 3903.0889898688915, 639.0, 365.0 ],
                    "text": "To automate the mask, in addition to the real-time mask calculation using a recorded video (e.g. a webcam feed from the aforesaid D4.demo.mask.sensel&webcam) which can prove to be CPU intensive, you can use the D4.mask.editor in conjuction with the D4.mask.renderer which is capable of storing a series of masks at specified timestamps into an array of jit.matrices, as well as rendering the final mask for a particular loudspeaker configuration and store it in a simple coll file that is considerably less computationally intensive. The source matrices can be stored as a set of jxf files together with the session data consisting of the value pairs denoting the time point (and the name) of each matrix snapshot, followed by the xfade option (whether transitioning from the previous matrix onto the next one should be achieved smoothly or instantly when the next keyframe timepoint is reached). Both the jxf matrices and the session data are stored in a folder specified in the D4.mask.renderer's \"write matrix keyframe session\" entry. The same can be loaded by entering the same folder name inside the \"read keyframe session\". One such simple session is included in the renderer-example folder.\n\nTo be able to use the D4.mask.renderer, it needs to be combined with the D4.mask.editor which will provide visual feedback of the current mask at a specified timestamp, as well as a D4.calc~-example patch that provides the context, or the \"test\" bus. You will need to specify this bus in both the D4.mask.renderer and the D4.mask.editor widgets before the mask will have any effect. Once everything is set up, you can use the \"timeline scrub\" value to reviewing timestamps 0-2 that are included in the demo session.\n\nTo add new keyframe, use the \"store frame\" to indicate the keyframe's timestamp. Don't forget to indicate whether you would like to have the crossfading enabled between the newly stored keyframe and the next keyframe.\n\nLastly, you can also render the current mask automation to a specific loudspeaker configuration. See the \"render timeline\" set of number boxes and buttons where you can indicate which frame ranges you wish to render, time increments between the frames as well as the duration of each increment in milliseconds. You can start rendering by clicking on the \"render\" button. Once the rendering is complete, the \"done\" button will light up and you will find a coll file in your project file with the same name as the bus, e.g. test.coll."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-67",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 3881.0889898688915, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Playback of the Rendered Automation"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-66",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 4868.448813268449, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Playback of the Rendered Automation"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-65",
                    "linecount": 2,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 7906.448813268449, 143.0, 34.0 ],
                    "text": "D4\nD4_med_matrix.class",
                    "textjustification": 2
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-64",
                    "linecount": 27,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 7456.448813268449, 631.0, 379.0 ],
                    "text": "To date D4 has been utilized in a broad array of scenarios, ranging from scientific to purely artistic. Below are several examples that highlight D4's potential, all of which are either final products or excerpts from a larger body of work.\n\nD4.demo.moogfest2017 and its binaural variant D4.demo.moggfest2017.binaural are patches specifically designed for a Moogfest 2017 workshop and its custom audio system. It showcases a way to stack multiple D4.calc~ objects, automate their spatial data, and treat them as synchronized stems. While its spatial content may not be compatible with your output, it can be easily retrofitted to utilize the binaural mesh approach.\n\nD4.demo.mask.sensel&webcam offers a demonstration of live mask input from either webcam or the Sensel controller. Latter was utilized in a composition Envelop (2018) that utilizes Sensel's ability to sense touch and produce a grayscale matrix of the current surface touch pressure. You can learn more about the Sensel (and Locus used by the Traces below) new interfaces for spatial musical expression in the following New Interfaces for Musical Expression 2020 international conference publication.\n\nD4.demo.sonification.data.solar (2019) and its binaural version D4.demo.sonification.data.solar.binaural offers a glimpse at the spatialization potential of the D4's spatial mask. In this case, it focuses on sonifying atmospheric data during a quiescent Earth's day. The research in sonification of the solar data was in part supported by the United States National Science Foundation research grant. The same sonification excerpt is also used as the intro of the Traces work below.\n\nTraces (2019) is an interactive work whose primary focus is on the spatial dimension. It is also the most ambitious example of utilizing the D4 library inside Max. As indicated in the \"Threading and Optimization\" section above, its live production called for over 25K DSP calls and 8K audio streams being mixed down to 130 physical outputs live using 21.3ms latency (1024 sample buffer at 48KHz). This project was made possible through a careful use of Max's poly~ object that allows for toggling of DSP processing in individual loudspeakers based on whether that particular loudspeaker in a given bus has any meaningful output. See the \"Threading and Optimization\" section above for more info on the D4-supported optimization strategies."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-63",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 7431.448813268449, 125.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 Use Examples",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-62",
                    "linecount": 23,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5233.448813268449, 628.0, 324.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 library also comes with several examples of a custom and arguably more advanced approaches to spatializing sound. This is particularly important because while drawing a smiley spatial mask with the D4.mask.editor may be fun, it, by default, will not sound like anything special. Much like the amplitude panning, the same sound emanating from multiple loudspeakers and with varying amplitudes will effectively fuse into a single source, thereby losing any semblance of a smiley's eyes, or its smile. This is where custom approaches to modulating individual channel outputs inside the D4.calc~ abstraction provide a whole new level of complexity and virtually limitless opportunities for researching new approaches to rendering complex visual data using spatial sound. Note how the D4.calc~ has two creation arguments. First (required) sets its bus name or context. The second, optional one allows the user to specify a custom per-channel abstraction. By default D4.calc~ uses D4.cell~ abstraction to process each channel's output by utilizing a basic amplitude modulation based on the D4's calculated amplitude and ramp up and ramp down setting. Therefore creating an object \"D4.calc~ test\" is equal to creating a \"D4.calc~ test D4.calc~\". Substituting this abstraction with other implementations allows for more complex manipulation of the outgoing sound.\n\nAn example of advanced processing can be experienced by opening both D4.calc~-example-custom and D4.mask.editor.custom. Note how this instance of D4.calc~ uses the D4.cell.mask2phasor~ abstraction for each of its channels. To explore how amplitude modulation of individual loudspeakers is affected by the incoming amplitude, clear out the mask by pressing the 'C' button, and then draw dots of different sizes and opacities in different areas of the spatial mask (click on the widget's \"help\" button to learn how to adjust size and opacity). Make sure to listen to each (you may want to focus on smaller set of loudspeakers, such as channels 1 and 2 to be able to hear them or use Max's audio I/O mapping to be able to hear loudspeakers outside your available number of loudspeakers) channel separately. Note also options inside the D4.calc~-example-custom by which you can affect the shape of the modulating waveform. This is just the beginning of what may be possible with the spatial mask and there is a growing body of research currently exploring the extent of its utility in the area of immersive sonification and beyond."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-55",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5559.448813268449, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Mask and Radius"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "id": "obj-46",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5211.448813268449, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "Advanced Output Processing"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "handoff": "",
                    "id": "obj-85",
                    "maxclass": "ubutton",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 4,
                    "outlettype": [ "bang", "bang", "", "int" ],
                    "parameter_enable": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 211.0, 359.0, 36.0, 17.5 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "hidden": 1,
                    "id": "obj-84",
                    "linecount": 3,
                    "maxclass": "message",
                    "numinlets": 2,
                    "numoutlets": 1,
                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                    "patching_rect": [ 373.33333333333337, 337.5, 263.0, 50.0 ],
                    "text": ";\r\nmax launch_browser https://smartech.gatech.edu/handle/1853/56592"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-83",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 209.0, 357.5, 40.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "paper",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-81",
                    "linecount": 19,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 329.5, 629.0, 269.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 was motivated by the introduction of the Layer Based Amplitude Panning (LBAP) algorithm for spatializing audio. It is the first and the most comprehensive implementation of LBAP to date. To learn more about reasons for LBAP's introduction, check out the following paper. \n\nThere are several important considerations that have inspired the LBAP's implementation, and with it the D4 library. LBAP's approach to the human auditory spatial perception is exocentric or world stabilized. In other words, it mimics the way we interact with the real world. Namely, our spatial auditory perception is inextricably linked with other senses, such as the sense of orientation, location, and motion. In our interaction with the real world there is no such thing as a sweet spot--when we look for our ringing smartphone, we continually adjust our location and orientation to improve our ability to locate a particular audio source. The same is true for the concert seating. If your seat is near a loudspeaker, no matter the complexity of the spatialization algorithm, what you will hear will be primarily that loudspeaker whose amplitude will commonly overpower other, more distant loudspeakers. As such, LBAP treats the perimeter of the auditory space delineated by the loudspeaker array (commonly a sphere- or dome-like shape) as an aural canvas. And much like studying a large painting, or the previous smartphone searching scenario, where user's location is often critical in allowing them to locate the smartphone, as users move around the space, their perception of the ensuing content is heavily influenced by their location and orientation.\n\nYet, for those seeking the sweet spot, the same can be still aptly delivered as long as the listener is placed in or near the center of the space, which is, by default, true of its binaural mesh approach."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-80",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 306.0, 461.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "LBAP -- A Different Approach to Immersion and Audio Spatialization",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-79",
                    "linecount": 45,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 6773.448813268449, 639.0, 627.0 ],
                    "text": "As of version 2.4.0, D4 has introduced another first-of-a-kind feature--the binaural mesh. Whereas traditional approaches to virtualizing sound using headphone-based binauralization of the audio source utilizes one binaural pathway or plugin per source, the binaural mesh creates an array of binaural pathways or plugins for each virtualized loudspeaker, as defined by the currently instantiated D4's loudspeaker calibration. This approach offers a number of unique advantages over its traditional counterparts. First, it allows for a more accurate rendering of a venue it aims to simulate, including the way radius propagates across various loudspeakers, any unique coloring of various loudspeakers, a finer control over special loudspeakers such as subwoofers, avoidance of potentially inconsistent coloring of the sound as it transitions from one position to another due to limitations of the parametric and/or convolution-based mapping, as well as simulation of other potential idiosynchrasies of the simulated space and the underlying spatialization algorithm, such as phasing between adjacent loudspeakers rendering the same sound. The same also allows for the use of D4's unique affordances, such as the spatial mask, as well as a battery of its supporting tools and widgets. It offers seamless transition from a physical to virtual, requiring only the recalibration of the virtual loudspeaker positions and replacing of the D4.dac~ with a poly~-based virtual outputs. Each such virtual loudspeaker further enables per-loudspeaker enhancements, e.g. per-loudspeaker modulation of the audio signal that goes beyond the usual parametric options offered by the common binaural tools and plugins, something that may prove particularly useful in the area of immersive sonification research.\n\nThe D4's approach to binaural reproduction of spatial audio sources is also uniquely modular. By default, it relies on a free Sennheiser's Ambeo Orbit plugin. With minor adaptation of the provided example abstraction, this design choice allows users to experiment with different plugins and their approaches (e.g. parametric versus convolution-based) to binaural rendering of sources.\n\nLastly, D4's binaural mesh has a unique ability to scale. It instantiates all the outputs once to be used by as many of audio sources or D4.calc~ instances. Once the outputs are instantiated, additional sources generate minimal computational overhead. This, however, also proves to be its one notable shortcoming. Depending on the number of virtual loudspeakers, rendering even a single source may prove significantly more computationally demanding than a traditional approach to binaural rendering of the same that utilizes only one binaural plugin. As of 2021, D4 library has been able to render up to 32 virtual loudspeakers using this approach. Given the modular nature of its loudspeaker calibration mechanism, this may mean adapting some of the spaces with a large number of loudspeakers to a sparser simulated virtual binaural environment.\n\nTo explore the binaural mesh, open the D4.calc~-example-binaural patch. Please make sure to read the annotations that go over the steps required to ensure that the patch works as expected. Note the D4.calc~-example-binaural is effectively identical to that of a D4.calc~-example that renders audio to physical outputs with one notable difference: instead of utilizing D4.dac~ outputs, it relies on the poly~ object that invokes as many instances, or \"voices\", of the D4.dac.cell.binaural.ambeo~ abstraction as there are virtual outputs. Note that in order to be able to attain higher number of virtual outputs (e.g. 24 or 32), you may need to enable parallel processing and use multiple threads that are associated with the poly~ object. See the poly~ help file for additional information on how those function. In addition, note how this particular instance relies on the aforesaid Sennheiser's free Ambeo Orbit VST plugin and that with some adaptation the D4.dac.cell.binaural.ambeo~ abstraction can be easily adapted to support other plugins. To visually monitor binaural output, you can use either the D4.3D.visualizer~ or its 2D counterpart D4.meter.monitor.binaural~.\n\nAgain, note that the spatial audio virtualization inside D4 using the aforesaid binaural mesh approach is fully compatible with other D4 library's features, including the spatial mask, and various audio visualization widgets. So, make sure to experiment with those, as well."
                }
            },
            {
