                    "text": "Both rendered mask and other autmoation data files can be played back by the D4.player abstraction. As soon as you indicate the bus inside the D4.player (which can be also used as a regular non-gui abstraction inside your main patch and can be given first optional argument to set the bus--see inlet and outlet annotation for the explanation of their function), the mask and other (a.k.a. pos) data toggle buttons on the right side of the D4.player widget will light up in case the object is able to locate the mask and/or other data files in its path. For the non-gui iteration, the third and fourth outlets will output 1 (found) or 0 (not found) for the mask and pos files, respectively. Note that D4.player does not work with the raw session mask data found in the session subfolder. Instead, it only works with the rendered mask data found in the coll file and is envisioned as the CPU optimized and loudspeaker setup specific playback abstraction. Note that D4.player needs to work in conjunction with the D4.calc~ that has the same bus name as the saved data. To hear the resulting sound, you will need to have the D4.calc~-example up and running and paired with the D4.player.\n\nD4.player can be also affected by a global or per-instance azimuth and elevation offset. This may be particularly useful, once you have created a spatial auditory scene using prerendered data, to simulate listener's ability to orient themselves inside the auditory scene. To do this, you can use d4-glob-azim and d4-glob-elev sends that are by default enabled on all D4.player abstractions. If you wish to disable global offset on a particular D4.player instance, you can give it second optional argument (0 or 1). 0 (default) will keep the global azimuth and elevation values enabled, while 1 will disable them for that particular instance. You can also control individual D4.player instance based on its bus name by sending values to d4-<bus>-azim and d4-<bus>-elevation. For instance, a bus with a name of test would have these automatically set to d4-test-azim and d4-test-elev."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-74",
                    "linecount": 5,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5581.448813268449, 628.0, 75.0 ],
                    "text": "Consider interesting opportunities for experimentation by combinbing the mask and radius (a.k.a. spread) values. Think of the radius as a search light centered around the current source's azimuth and elevation point, while mask denoting areas that are actually reflective. Combining the two can create unique shapes, like curved lines that keep evolving as the azimuth, elevation, radius, and mask change and evolve over time. Experiment with the automation in combination with more advanced per-channel modulation strategies that go beyond amplitude."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-61",
                    "linecount": 5,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 3804.0889898688915, 628.0, 75.0 ],
                    "text": "In addition to live processing, D4 includes a set of abstractions that support the automation and storing of the automation data. The automation can be split into two components:\n\n1) the spatial mask, and\n2) other parameters (azimuth, elevation, radius, gain, ramp up, and ramp down)."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-60",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 5186.448813268449, 144.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "Advanced Features",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-58",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 2068.8375, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "D4.3D.visualizer~ Widget",
                    "textjustification": 1
                }
            },
