                    "text": "Spatial mask is first-of-a-kind approach to spatializing audio. It is effectively an aural counterpart to the image masking. At its core is the D4.calc~ (and D4 external's) ability to assign a mask to each outputting channel. If the mask is 0, the maximum possible amplitude on that channel is going to be 0, regardless of incoming amplitude. Mask of 0.5 will allow the maximum amplitude to be 0.5. So, with the mask of 0.5, if the incoming amplitude is 0.25, it the outgoing one will be also 0.25, the incoming amplitude of 0, will have outgoing amplitude of also 0. However, an amplitude of 0.6 (or any other number greater than 0.5) will be clipped down to 0.5.\n\nThe spatial mask can be applied using messages that are sent to the rightmost inlet of the D4.calc~ abstraction. see the D4.calc~-example patch for a convenient instance you can experiment with. This, however, is not necessarily the most efficient way of creating complex mask. This is why D4 comes with the D4.mask.editor widget that also uses busses to communicate with various instances of the D4.calc~ abstraction. This widget allows you to \"paint\" the grayscale mask which is then automatically applied to the desired bus using your mouse (white areas having the highest value of 1 and black areas having the lowest value of 0), or, generate mask using an external input, such as the webcam or the sensel controller. See D4.demo.mask.sensel&webcam for examples of the last two approaches. Note that the spatial mask cannot be applied to the main output and this is why the mask's default bus inside the D4.mask.editor is set to \"null\". Please click on the \"help\" button inside the D4.mask.editor widget to learn more about the mask editor controls.\n\nBy default, the spatial mask is calculated as a rectangular area at a mid-point between the loudspeaker it belongs to and the adjacent loudspeakers. Its value is the computed average brightness of this area. Experiment with the D4.mask.editor to observe mask's effect on the resulting loudspeaker amplitude.\n\nNote that the mask for all output channels is by default 1. This is why when you connect a D4.mask.editor to a new unedited bus, its background becomes completely white. Note how it also gets populated with loudspeaker locations with red dots denoting regular layers, while green dots solo layers. When using the D4.mask.editor in conjunction with the D4.3D.visualizer~, the D4.3D.visualizer~ is also capable of showing the mask in the 3D space, as shown in the image in the \"Busses and Monitoring\" section above.\n\nLastly, while using the D4.mask.editor in live settings with or without the visualization widget is possible (see D4.demo.mask.sensel&webcam for an example), in high density loudspeaker scenarios it can quickly become computationally intensive. As such, its use needs to be carefully tested in live performance settings."
                }
            },
