            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-11",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 2105.8375, 192.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "Loudspeaker Configuration",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-20",
                    "linecount": 59,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 2130.8375, 637.0, 821.0 ],
                    "text": "In addition to the Virginia Tech Cube's 124.4.2 default loudspeaker setup embedded in the D4.calc~ abstraction's \"default_layer_setup\" subpatch, D4 offers a growing number of other configurations that can be loaded by adding the desired D4.speaker.calibration.* abstraction to your patch and sending a bang or a loadbang signal into its only inlet. Doing so will reconfigure all instances of the D4.calc~ abstraction, as well as all the supporting visualization widgets. While the D4.calc~ abstraction is envisioned to be rather flexible and extensible and its editing should be generally avoided, don't forget you can also edit it to accommodate other non-standard customizations.\n\nThe configuration file has a relatively simple structure that allows for the creation of a virtually unlimited number of other loudspeaker configurations, all of which can be switched live by sending a bang signal to an appropriate abstraction. Note that when a new configuration is loaded, you will likely need to use a different 2D visualizer that accurately represents locations of its loudspeakers. Again, D4 comes with a growing number of prebuilt D4.meter.monitor.* 2D visualizers for this purpose, and just like the configuration itself, building new visualizers using the D4.meter.cell~ and other abstractions should be a fairly straightforward effort. You may want to use an existing visualizer as your foundation, particularly considering it will also include the bus switching component, the main output volume controller, as well as a bpatcher version of the D4.meter.cell~.\n\nTo create your own custom calibration you will want to embed the following commands into a message object that is being sent to the d4-setup global send. The commands need to be presented in the exact order presented below, with each line being separated by a comma:\n\n1) establish loudspeaker layers (as per the LBAP standard where loudspeakers are grouped into horizontal layers) using the \"setup\" command, followed by a list of elevations for each of the layers. For instance, \"setup 0 30 60 90\" (without quotes) will tell D4 that there are 4 layers of loudspeakers, one at 0° elevation, one at 30°, and two more at 60° and 90° elevations, respectively. Note that these need to be provided starting with the lowest elevation and going to highest. Also note that only values between -90 and 90 is allowed, a range that covers all possible elevations in a spherical system.\n\n2) use the command \"layer\" to provide output channel and azimuth pairs for all the loudspeakers assigned to a layer, starting with the lowest one first. Note that each layer needs to be given a number (first argument following the word \"layer\"), starting with the layer 0. In addition, loudspeakers need to be ordered by their azimuth value, starting with the one closest to the azimuth of 0° and then progressing clockwise from there. For instance, \"layer 1 1 0 2 90 3 180 7 270\" would mean the values refer to the 2nd layer (its first argument is 1, whereas the first layer would have the first argument 0), followed by the information on 4 loudspeakers: channel 1 has the azimuth of 0°, channel 2 90°, channel 3 180° and channel 7 270°. Note that channel numbers do not need to be sequential. Do this for each layer.\n\n3) Optionally, you may also want to add one or more solo layers using the \"solo\" command. This is usually needed for the subwoofer layers which typically do not concern themselves with the source elevation. Such layers are still susceptible to the azimuth in the event the layer has more than one loudspeaker. Otherwise, the single loudspeaker will output full amplitude regardless the source azimuth and elevation. The format for the \"solo\" command is the same as for the layer, except that it does not require the solo layer number considering that each solo layer captures the entire immersive sphere. For instance, \"solo 17 0 18 180\" suggests that there are 2 loudspeakers, one using channel 17 at azimuth of 0°, and another on channel 18 located at the azimuth 180°.\n\n4) Lastly, finish the calibration data by sending the \"end\" command which has no supporting arguments.\n\nBelow is a simple example of an older iteration of the Virginia Tech DISIS' 16.2 loudspeaker setup, consisting of 2 8-channel perimeter layers, one at the 0° elevation and another at the 19° elevation, and a 2-channel sub solo layer:\n\nsetup 0 19, layer 0 2 22.5 3 67.5 4 112.5 5 157.5 6 202.5 7 247.5 8 292.5 1 337.5, layer 1 9 0 10 45 11 90 12 135 13 180 14 225 15 270 16 315, solo 17 0 18 180, end\n\nConsidering that each D4.calc~ can have only one set of homogeneous layers with multiple elevations, advanced setups with two non-homogeneous multi-elevation loudspeaker layers may require the use of two parallel D4.calc~ objects, one for each set. In addition, note how creating dummy layers may allow for sounds to fade out in specific areas. For instance, check out the D4.speaker.calibration.icat.cube.nofloor which introduces a layer with a dummy speaker channel below the lowest layer and thereby renders any sound below the horizon increasingly softer and eventually silent. This will effectively render the lower hemisphere of the cube silent instead of cross-fading the sound across the floor when its elevation goes (for instance) from 0° to -180°, thereby transitioning from one end of the room towards the other."
                }
            },
