                    "patching_rect": [ 498.5, 982.5, 100.0, 13.5 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-29",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 497.0, 979.5, 105.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "D4.calc~-example",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-24",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 939.5, 79.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 Basics",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-22",
                    "linecount": 20,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 1010.5, 635.0, 282.0 ],
                    "text": "Central to this setup is the D4.calc~ abstraction that allows for spatialization of a single positional source, including all of its more advanced features, such as ramp up and down values, radius, and spatial mask. By default, it leverages the D4.cell~ abstraction (which can be overriden using an optional argument) that is responsible for calculating per-loudspeaker output mix and loads the Virginia Tech Cube's 124.4.2 spatial audio output setup.\n\nThe D4.dac~ abstraction creates all the output nodes and connects them to Max's dac~ (in this case we use 139 channels because of the way the channels are mapped inside the Cube, with some channels remaining unused).\n\nThe D4.master bpatcher offers universal master output level control. As a result, the user does not have to go through a potentially laborious patching of each individual channel from each individual source, while the aforesaid\nD4.cell~ abstraction allows for a powerful per-channel manipulation. We will look at this and other advanced features further below.\n\nD4.calc~ offers two arguments. The first one is the \"bus\" under which you wish to place this particular source. This bus name is used throughout the D4 library. This way, if one wishes to visualize the audio output on a particular bus versus the main mix, they can do so by specifying such bus in the supporting monitoring/visualizing widget. You may want to look inside the D4.calc~ abstraction to learn more about all the messages it supports and the way it behaves. We will revisit each of these further below. Note that opening the D4.calc~ abstraction by itself outside the context of one of the example patches will generate errors due to the dynamic patching nature of the D4 library and that this is perfectly normal behavior."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-17",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 979.5, 628.0, 20.0 ],
                    "text": "A more elaborate version of the patch that describes each feature in detail can be found in D4.calc~-example."
                }
            },
