                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "handoff": "",
                    "id": "obj-18",
                    "maxclass": "ubutton",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 4,
                    "outlettype": [ "bang", "bang", "", "int" ],
                    "parameter_enable": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 123.66666666666663, 836.5, 107.0, 14.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "hidden": 1,
                    "id": "obj-19",
                    "linecount": 2,
                    "maxclass": "message",
                    "numinlets": 2,
                    "numoutlets": 1,
                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                    "patching_rect": [ 259.5, 817.5, 221.0, 36.0 ],
                    "text": ";\r\nmax launch_browser http://java.com/en/"
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-16",
                    "linecount": 20,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 640.5, 632.0, 282.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 library folder and its authorization file are envisioned to be used as a self-standing folder as part of a project. You can have as many of these folders on the same computer. Even if you are dual-booting Windows and OSX using Bootcamp, it will work on both OSs. The reason for using D4 this way instead of dropping it into the Max library folder is because of its mostly-open-source approach that encourages users to extend the library's functionality. For instance, your project may require you to modify a number of the D4 abstractions. This way, making these changes will not irreversibly alter one and only installation folder. That said, some users opt to use D4 as-is and end up installing it in the Max library folder with the expectation that they will not alter its abstractions, and that is perfectly fine, as well. No worries. If you ever corrupt your install, just download the latest version from the website linked above and copy the authorization file and you'll be good to go. When updating, you can simply overwrite the folder (assuming you have not altered the abstractions), or create another folder on your computer.\n\nPlease note that the D4.mask.renderer patch responsible for saving and rendering animated masks utilizes D4_med_matrix Java object. While this object is not critical for the core D4 operations, if you decide to explore this aspect of the D4 library, you will need to have Java installed on your computer. To download latest version of Java for your computer visit https://java.com/en/.\n\nLastly, for its binaural mesh virtualization of spatial sources, D4 is built to leverage any existing binaural plugin. By default, it utilizes Sennheiser's free Ambeo plugin that needs to be installed and added to the Max path. To download the Ambeo plugin, visit https://en-us.sennheiser.com/ambeo-orbit. Don't forget to set your Max path properties to ensure that Max sees the installed VST plugin. We will discuss the virtualization further below."
                }
            },
