                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-7",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 78.0, 93.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "Introduction",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 1,
                    "fontsize": 14.0,
                    "id": "obj-6",
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 615.5, 111.0, 23.0 ],
                    "text": "Getting Started",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.666666666666667, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-4",
                    "linecount": 13,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 103.0, 628.0, 186.0 ],
                    "text": "D4 is a set of objects and abstractions designed to provide cutting-edge tools for spatializing audio content, as well as to researching new approaches to spatialization and immersion. It is particularly geared towards high density loudspeaker arrays and live low-latency spatialization. It leverages layer based amplitude panning (LBAP) spatialization algorithm that was developed in conjunction with the library. By leveraging Max's Jitter library, the D4 library offers first-of-a-kind spatial mask feature. It is also the first library to offer the binaural mesh approach to virtualizing spatial content. If you are interested solely in the LBAP algorithm, you may want to look into the free IRCAM Spat library that (among others) also offers the LBAP approach to spatializing sound.\n\nTo learn more about the science behind the D4 library, visit http://ico.bukvic.net/main/d4post/.\n\nThe library defaults to a demo mode that is fully functional for up to 4 minutes and 33 seconds after which the D4 external object will cease to work. To purchase a license and thereby disable the demo mode, visit\nhttp://ico.bukvic.net/main/d4/."
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-41",
                    "linecount": 31,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 16.0, 1334.5, 632.0, 434.0 ],
                    "text": "Instead of a monolithic design (everything inside a single patch), D4 uses a series of abstractions or widgets that leverage D4's bus system for monitoring, visualization, and recording. While by default they are used as separate visual objects, they also have their own inlets that allow for easy embedding into larger patches as non-graphical abstractions.\n\nIn the D4.calc~-example-minimal patch note the blue colored D4.meter.monitor.cube~ 2D output visualizer (the same can be also opened using the afroesaid link) that corresponds with the default speaker mapping of the Virginia Tech Cube's 124.4.2 spatial audio setup. Observe how when you monitor the main output (default, with the bus text entry being empty), it is affected by the output levels specified by the D4.master slider's output level setting. If you set the monitoring bus to \"test\" by entering \"test\" (without quotes) in the bus text entry box, note how now the output remains constant regardless of the main output levels.\n\nD4 comes with a growing number of other 2D visualization widgets, including other Virginia Tech spaces, as well as more common surround sound setups. One can easily build other configurations using the D4.meter.cell~ inside a bpatcher (you can simply copy ones found in the existing visualizers and then by adjusting their creation arguments. You will find additional information on its creation arguments by clicking on the link to the patch above.\n\nNow, with the D4.calc~-example open and outputting audio, load the D4.3D.visualizer~. Note how the smaller D4.3D.visualizer~ patch widget offers an orange help button. Click on that button to learn more how to navigate the supporting 3D visualization window. By default, the visualizer loads the loudspeaker setup associated with the main output. Each loudspeaker will be denoted by a red sphere. As loudspeaker's output amplitude increases, so does the sphere's radius. Note how you can once again use the bus option to determine which audio bus you wish monitor. Don't forget to look around using the mouse click and drag and investigate where your loudspeakers are positioned inside the space and how they interact with the spatialized content. In addition to amplitude, the D4.3D.visualizer~ is also capable of displaying the current mask. We will look into the mask further below.\n\nPlease note that D4.3D.visualizer~ visualizes all the loudspeakers in a spherical equidistant fashion from the center of the generic cuboid space. As a result, in its current version, the D4.3D.visualizer~ is not able to place individual loudspeakers outside of this imaginary sphere. As such, this visualization is being used mainly as a space-agnostic reference, rather than a definitive virtual version of the actual space. Given the modular nature of its implementation, creating a more accurate visualization of a specific space is possible."
                }
            }
        ],
